CAT = "rsracCtlInstall"; SEG = "CTI"
Q = [
("On a common 24 V thermostat, which terminal usually calls the cooling contactor?",
 "Y", ["W", "G", "R"],
 "Y energizes the compressor contactor, G the indoor fan, W the heat, and R is the 24 V supply from the transformer."),
("A heat pump thermostat has an O terminal. What does O usually energize?",
 "Reversing valve", ["Auxiliary heat strips", "Outdoor fan motor only", "Defrost board reset input"],
 "An O terminal energizes the reversing valve in cooling; a B terminal energizes it in heating. The equipment maker sets which one the unit uses."),
("Why does a 24 V control transformer have a secondary fuse or breaker?",
 "Protects it from shorts", ["It lowers the supply voltage", "It filters radio noise", "It stops the compressor cycling"],
 "A shorted thermostat wire or coil would overload the small transformer. A fuse on the secondary protects it and the control wiring."),
("Why is the common (C) wire run to a smart thermostat?",
 "Steady 24 V power", ["Calls for the indoor fan", "Switches the reversing valve", "Grounds the furnace cabinet"],
 "C completes the 24 V circuit so the thermostat's electronics have steady power without stealing current through the call circuits."),
("Where should the sensing bulb of a thermostatic expansion valve be fastened?",
 "Suction line at the coil outlet", ["Liquid line ahead of the valve", "Discharge line at the compressor", "Inside the drain pan"],
 "The bulb senses suction temperature leaving the evaporator so the valve can hold superheat. It is clamped tight, insulated, and placed on a horizontal run away from traps."),
("On a horizontal suction line of 22 mm diameter, where is a TXV bulb usually placed?",
 "4 or 8 o'clock", ["6 o'clock, underneath", "On a vertical riser", "On the liquid line"],
 "Placing the bulb on the side keeps it in good contact and away from the oil that runs along the bottom. Larger lines use the lower side positions; small ones often use the top."),
("Why is a low-pressure switch wired into a compressor's control circuit?",
 "Stops it on low suction", ["Raises suction pressure", "Starts the defrost heater", "Controls the indoor fan speed"],
 "A low-pressure cut-out opens when suction pressure falls too far, protecting the compressor from running with too little refrigerant or a starved coil."),
("A head pressure control is installed on an air-cooled condenser that runs in winter. What does it do?",
 "Holds head pressure up in cold", ["Lowers head pressure in summer", "Defrosts the evaporator", "Stops refrigerant migration"],
 "In cold weather, condensing pressure falls so far that the expansion device cannot feed properly. Fan cycling, fan speed control or flooding valves keep it in range."),
("Which wiring practice keeps low-voltage control cable from picking up interference?",
 "Keep it apart from line-voltage wiring", ["Run it in the same conduit as power", "Coil the extra length tightly", "Use a larger gauge than needed"],
 "Control and communication cables run apart from power conductors, or are shielded, so induced voltage does not cause false signals."),
("Why must the shield of a communication cable be grounded at one end only?",
 "Avoids a ground loop", ["To double the signal voltage", "To power the sensor", "To meet the colour code"],
 "Grounding a shield at both ends can create a current loop between ground points, which adds noise. One-end grounding drains interference without a loop."),
("A building automation system connects to a chiller controller. Which protocol is widely used for this?",
 "BACnet", ["HDMI", "Bluetooth audio", "USB mass storage"],
 "BACnet is an open building automation protocol used to share points and alarms between controllers from different makers."),
("Why is a duct static pressure sensor mounted about two-thirds down the longest duct run?",
 "Represents the far branches", ["It is closest to the fan", "Air is coldest at that point", "It avoids the filter bank"],
 "Variable air volume fans are controlled from a static pressure reading. Placing the sensor well down the main duct gives a value that keeps the farthest boxes supplied."),
("When replacing an old mercury bulb thermostat, how is the old one handled?",
 "Hazardous waste recycling", ["Put in household garbage", "Kept in the wall cavity", "Broken to recover the bulb"],
 "Mercury thermostats are hazardous waste. Many provinces run take-back programs, and the mercury must not be released or thrown in the garbage."),
("Why does a two-stage thermostat need a second cooling call wire?",
 "To call the high stage on its own", ["To power the thermostat display", "To switch the reversing valve", "To start the outdoor fan only"],
 "A two-stage system runs low stage on Y1 and adds high stage on Y2, so the thermostat needs a conductor for each call."),
("A retrofit replaces a pneumatic thermostat with a direct digital controller. What converts its signal for the old valve actuator?",
 "Electronic-to-pneumatic transducer", ["Pneumatic-to-electronic transducer", "Step-down control transformer", "Current sensing relay"],
 "An E/P transducer turns the controller's electrical output into a proportional air pressure, letting existing pneumatic actuators stay in service during a retrofit."),
("Why is a freeze stat installed across the face of a hydronic heating coil in an air handler?",
 "Stops it before freezing", ["Controls supply air humidity", "Measures outdoor air flow", "Starts the economizer"],
 "A capillary freeze stat strung across the coil face senses cold spots and stops the fan or closes outdoor dampers before water in the coil can freeze."),
]
